TOTK: Ampere jobs keep a memory load in place when a store may come between it and its use. The AST optimizer
(StructuredIr/AstOptimizer.cs Optimize, as in Ryujinx) moves an expression with one use to that use, a load too, and so
past later stores to the same memory; the runtime asks for that optimizer ("debug": false), seedds/nxnative's build does
not (its translations run in DebugMode by default). OoT's NDVE network shaders stage their weights through one
shared buffer, block after block (STS.128, then LDSM, as one warp, with no barrier): every LDSM load moved after the last
store, every HMMA group used the last block's weights, and the title movie and the 3D frame came out as noise. With
StructuredProgramContext.OrderedMemoryLoads (set with the Ampere flag) an expression that reads shared, local, storage or
global memory, outputs or storage images moves only when no store, atomic, barrier, call, fragment interlock or vertex
emission that may write that memory lies between it and its use (AstOptimizer.MutableReads, WritesMemory, UseFollows).
Shared and local arrays are told apart by id; storage and global memory count as one (two storage slots can be one
buffer, as in that network). Maxwell translations are unchanged.

